2429962158203125, 0.0022754669189453125, 0.01209259033203125, 0.052581787109375, 0.055633544921875, 0.03741455078125, 0.01361083984375, 0.0241851806640625, -0.01342010498046875, -0.039031982421875, 0.00559234619140625, 0.00807952880859375, 0.0079345703125, -0.0294647216796875, -0.0003788471221923828, 0.028839111328125, -0.0143890380859375, -0.02099609375, -0.009613037109375, -0.011383056640625, 0.01401519775390625, -0.0128326416015625, -0.0165252685546875, -0.019500732421875, -0.01593017578125, 0.0109100341796875, -0.023406982421875, 0.027587890625, -0.0202178955078125, 0.01410675048828125, -0.0069732666015625, -0.0411376953125, -0.02276611328125, -0.00467681884765625, 0.0120391845703125, -0.0190887451171875, -0.01146697998046875, 0.045745849609375, -0.004695892333984375, 0.01306915283203125, -0.0165863037109375, -0.0098114013671875, 0.036376953125, -0.00946044921875, 0.0299835205078125, 0.006011962890625], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Recent publications have begun to explore the application of Geometric Algebra (GA) to the modeling, analysis, and control of dynamical systems, particularly in the context of electrical circuits. A central challenge in this area is the transformation of the ordinary differential equations that govern a system's behavior from the real domain to the Laplace domain, a process fundamental to system analysis and control design. While previous works have extended the Laplace transform to certain hypercomplex algebras, such as quaternions and Clifford algebras, a comprehensive definition within the framework of Geometric Algebra remains lacking. This gap limits the ability to fully leverage GA's expressive power in the analysis of dynamical systems.\n\nIn this work, we address this gap by introducing a definition of the Laplace transform specifically tailored for Geometric Algebras. Our approach generalizes existing definitions for hypercomplex algebras and provides a consistent framework for applying Laplace-domain techniques within GA. We focus on geometric algebras with signature (p, q) where p + q \u2264 5, which encompasses the most commonly used algebras in physics and engineering. This restriction is due to computational tractability and the prevalence of these signatures in practical applications.\n\nWe present the formal definition of the Laplace transform in GA and establish its key properties, including linearity, the shifting theorem, and the treatment of multivector-valued functions. As a main result, we demonstrate that our definition preserves the essential features of the classical Laplace transform while enabling its application to multivector-valued signals and systems. This advancement opens new avenues for the analysis and control of dynamical systems using the tools of Geometric Algebra."}}